<p>To get your business on Google Maps, search for it on Maps first. If a listing already exists, claim it. If it doesn't, add your business at <a href="https://business.google.com/add" target="_blank" rel="noopener">business.google.com/add</a>, then verify that you own or manage it. Google says a Business Profile lets you <a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/7039811?hl=en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">manage how you appear on Maps and Search at no charge</a>.</p>

<p>Getting listed is the easy part. Looking good once you're there comes down to accurate details, the right category, current hours, real photos and a website that backs it all up. Here's how to do each step, plus what to do when an old listing is stuck in somebody else's account.</p>

<h2>Check whether you're already on Google Maps</h2>

<p>Google can create listings on its own, and customers can suggest places too, so your business may be on the map even if you never set it up. Before you add anything, search your business name plus "Albuquerque" on Google Maps. You'll usually land in one of three spots:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>No listing:</strong> Add your business as new.</li>
  <li><strong>A listing nobody has verified:</strong> Claim it. Don't create a second one.</li>
  <li><strong>A verified listing you can't get into:</strong> Request access from the owner. There's more on that below.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to add your business to Google Maps</h2>

<p>If you don't see a listing, <a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/2911778?hl=en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google's setup steps</a> are short:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Sign in to a Google account you plan to keep. Use a business account if you have one, not an employee's personal login.</li>
  <li>Go to business.google.com/add and select "Add your business to Google."</li>
  <li>Enter your business details as the prompts ask for them.</li>
  <li>Choose a verification method and finish its steps.</li>
</ol>

<p>If your listing already exists and isn't verified, open it in Google Maps, select "Claim this business," then "Manage now," and pick a verification option.</p>

<h2>How Google verification works</h2>

<p>You don't get to pick from every option. <a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/7107242?hl=en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google decides which verification methods you can use</a>, and they can include a phone call or text, email, a live video call, a recorded video or a postcard. A few details can save you a headache:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Phone menus can block the code.</strong> Automated phone systems won't receive it, so the call or text needs to reach a real person.</li>
  <li><strong>Live video calls need you on site.</strong> They're only available during your location's business hours.</li>
  <li><strong>Leave your profile alone while you wait for a postcard.</strong> Changing your name, address or category, or asking for a new code, can stop the mailed code from working. Most codes arrive within 14 days, and they expire after 30.</li>
  <li><strong>Reviews take time.</strong> After you finish your steps, Google's review can take up to 5 business days.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What if someone else controls your listing?</h2>

<p>This comes up a lot with a former employee or a past marketing vendor. If a verified profile exists and you're authorized to manage the business, you can <a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/4566671?hl=en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">request ownership through Google</a>. Enter your business at business.google.com/add, choose it from the list and select Request Access. The current owner gets an email and has 3 days to respond. If they don't, you may be able to claim the profile, though that option isn't always available.</p>

<p>Service-area businesses without a physical location have to contact Google support for this instead. Once you're in, add a second manager, so one person leaving doesn't lock you out again. It's the same reason we tell people to <a href="/blog/how-long-to-build-business-website">gather their logins before a website project</a>.</p>

<h2>Do you need a storefront to get on Google Maps?</h2>

<p>No, but your address has to follow <a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/3038177?hl=en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google's guidelines</a>. If customers come to you, use your real location and keep permanent signage with your business name at that address. P.O. boxes, remote mailboxes and virtual offices don't qualify.</p>

<p>If you go to your customers, like a roofer based in Albuquerque who works in Rio Rancho, Bernalillo and Los Lunas, you're a service-area business. Hide your address from customers and list the areas you serve. Google says that area generally shouldn't reach farther than about 2 hours of driving time from where you're based, though it notes larger areas can fit some businesses.</p>

<h2>What to fill out so your profile looks good</h2>

<p>Google says <a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/7091?hl=en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">complete and accurate info</a> makes a business more likely to show up in local results. It also helps the person deciding between you and the shop down the street.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Business name:</strong> Use your real-world name and nothing extra. Stick to what's on your sign. Adding extras like "Best Plumber Albuquerque NM" after your name breaks the rules and puts your listing at risk.</li>
  <li><strong>Categories:</strong> Google's <a href="https://services.google.com/fh/files/helpcenter/official_gbp_best_practices_playbook_2026.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2026 best practices playbook</a> allows one primary category and up to nine more, but the guidelines say to use as few as possible. Pick the primary one that describes your core business, and don't add extras just to fill the slots.</li>
  <li><strong>Hours:</strong> List your regular customer-facing hours, and use special hours for holidays and other changes. If you close early during Balloon Fiesta or the State Fair, say so. If you only work by appointment, Google says you shouldn't list hours at all.</li>
  <li><strong>Phone and website:</strong> Use a number that reaches your location, and a link to your own site. Google doesn't allow numbers or links that redirect people through a referral.</li>
  <li><strong>Description:</strong> You get up to 750 characters, with no links or HTML. Say what you do, who you serve and which parts of the metro you cover, the way you'd explain it to a customer.</li>
  <li><strong>Photos:</strong> Add real shots of your storefront, your team and your work. They should be in focus and well lit, without heavy filters. Google's playbook says businesses that add photos get 42% more requests for directions on Maps.</li>
</ul>

<p>Vince got an early look at why this matters. Back in 2012 and 2013, at the New Mexico agency ExploreMedia360, he moderated 360 tours on Google Maps for clients including Art Gone Wild Galleries in Santa Fe. That meant stitching panoramas so people could move through them easily, and blurring faces and license plates. You can see those tours on <a href="/about">his about page</a>. A Maps listing is often somebody's first walk through your front door, so it's worth making that walk clear.</p>

<h2>Make your website back up your listing</h2>

<p>Your profile and your website should tell the same story. Keep your business name, address, phone number and hours the same on your site, your listing and your printed materials. If you move or change your number, update all of them on the same day.</p>

<p>Send your profile's website link to a page that loads fast on a phone and makes the next step obvious: call, book or get directions. A great listing that lands on an outdated page loses people at the last second. If your site is that weak link, our <a href="/blog/website-redesign-checklist-new-mexico">website redesign checklist</a> is a good place to start.</p>

<h2>Why isn't my business showing up on Google Maps?</h2>

<p>If you're brand new, start with the basics. Confirm your profile is verified, then check whether your latest edits are still in review. Google reviews changes before they go live.</p>

<p>If everything's live and you still can't find yourself, it's usually about ranking. Google's local results depend on <a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/7091?hl=en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">relevance, distance and prominence</a>. In other words, how well you match the search, how close you are to the person searching, and how well known you are. Someone searching from the Northeast Heights may see a different set of businesses than someone in the South Valley, and that's normal.</p>

<p>You can't buy your way up. Google says there's no way to request or pay for a better local ranking. What you can do is keep your profile accurate and earn reviews, since Google says more reviews and positive ratings can help. Ask happy customers directly with your <a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/3474122?hl=en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google review link or QR code</a>. Just never offer a discount or gift in exchange, because Google strictly prohibits that.</p>

<h2>Getting help with your online presence</h2>

<p>Setting up a Business Profile doesn't take long, and keeping it current only takes a little time here and there. The harder part is making sure the website behind it earns the click. Our <a href="/web-design">custom website design and development</a> work is built around that, from search friendly copy and structured data to pages that read beautifully on a phone. If you'd like a second set of eyes on your site, <a href="#contact">book a call</a> and tell us what you're seeing.</p>
